-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## Parameters
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Job parameters facilitate reusability and customization of job configurations, by allowing you to pass key/value pairs into your jobs to configure specific settings.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC For example, if we examine the notebook code for the **ingest_customermgmt** task, you'll notice there are no hard-coded names:
-- MAGIC    ```
-- MAGIC    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS ${catalog}.${wh_db}_${scale_factor}_stage.CustomerMgmt…
-- MAGIC    ```
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC SQL statements written like this is much more portable, since there are no hardcoded assumptions about the catalog or schema names; these can be passed in from the caller.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Databricks Lakeflow Jobs make it easy for you to define the parameters at the task or job level and reference those parameters in the executed code using the pattern `${parameter_name}`. As you can see, the parameters for the current tasks are pushed down from the job parameter. During execution, the referenced parameter will be replaced with the values set in the job task.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## Triggering
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC To unconditionally run the job at once, click the **Run now** button at the top right corner. This will run all the tasks in the job using the predetermined SQL warehouse. Once the entire job completes, you will be able to see the historical runs in the **Runs** tab, which lists all the previous executions, as well as the resulting status. This allows you to monitor and track the historical runs for performance improvements or error diagnostics.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Let's look at some alternate ways to trigger job execution.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### Time-based Scheduling
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC One common use case behind jobs is periodic batch processing. To address this requirement, jobs can be executed on a schedule.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. Refer to the **Schedules & Triggers** panel in the right sidebar. Click **Add trigger**.
-- MAGIC 1. Select a **Trigger Type** of *Scheduled*.
-- MAGIC 1. Depending on scheduling requirements, choose *Simple* or *Advanced* for **Schedule type**, then specify the schedule details.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### File Arrival
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Executing a job on arrival of new data is also a common use case, which Databricks accommodates.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. Refer to the **Schedules & Triggers** panel in the right sidebar. Click **Add trigger**.
-- MAGIC 1. Select a **Trigger Type** of *File arrival*.
-- MAGIC 1. Specify the location to monitor, in terms of a volume or external location, both of which are file-based securable objects in Unity Catalog.
-- MAGIC 1. If desired, use the **Advanced** options to implement throttling.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### Programmatic Triggering
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC There are a number of ways to trigger jobs programmatically. The cell below illustrates a simple Python excerpt which triggers the job using the [Databricks SDK for Python](https://databricks-sdk-py.readthedocs.io/en/latest/index.html).
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Note that the cell will take a few moments to execute since it waits for the execution to complete. To simply trigger the job asynchronously, we could instead replace the below API invocation with the following:
-- MAGIC    ```
-- MAGIC    client.jobs.run_now(job_id=job.job_id)
-- MAGIC    ```

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %python
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC from databricks.sdk import WorkspaceClient
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC # connect to the local workspace
-- MAGIC client = WorkspaceClient()
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC # locate the job (name ending with "-TPCDI")
-- MAGIC try:
-- MAGIC     job = next(
-- MAGIC         filter(
-- MAGIC             lambda j: j.settings.name.endswith('-TPCDI'),
-- MAGIC             client.jobs.list()
-- MAGIC         )
-- MAGIC     )
-- MAGIC except StopIteration:
-- MAGIC     raise ValueError("Job not found")
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC # trigger the job and wait for completion
-- MAGIC client.jobs.run_now_and_wait(job_id=job.job_id)

